   JAPANESE NEURAL NETWORK SOCIETY - PROCEEDINGS, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {1998},
Pages = {1016-1019},
Note = {5th International Conference on Neural Information Processing (ICONIP
   98) / 1998 Annual Conference of the Japanese-Neural-Network-Society
   (JNNS 98), KITAKYUSHU, JAPAN, OCT 21-23, 1998},
Organization = {Japanese Neural Network Soc; Asian Pacific Neural Network Assembly},
Abstract = {Porosity prediction is essential to predict hydrocarbon reserves.
   Recently, studies have indicated that multilayer perceptrons are the
   most promising tool to date in the problem domain, because of its
   ability to learn and generalize from a given data set. First, we show a
   multilayer perceptron trained with well log data from one well could
   predict the porosity measurements of another well, much better than a
   conventional multiple linear regression. Second, clustering techniques
   such as SOM and LVQ were applied to divide the problem into subproblems.
   The subproblems gave rise to a better prediction performance if the
   training data and test data have similar distributions. Finally,
   artificially generated ``virtual samples{''} were added to training
   sets. These expanded training data sets helped a committee of MLPs to
   achieve better prediction performance than a single MLP by about 35\%.},
