   TECH UNIV, TRABZON, TURKEY, JUL 26-29, 1998},
Organization = {Karadeniz Tech Univ, Turkey; King Fahd Univ Petr \& Minerals, Saudi
   Arabia; Univ Victoria, Canada; Univ Miami, US; Istanbul Tech Univ,
   Turkey},
Abstract = {The rates and locations of emission, the rates of diffusion and
   dispersion, the speed of physical transformation and reaction, and the
   processes governing deposition of aromatic Volatile Organic Compounds
   (VOC's) primarily from vehicle emissions are all factors which affect
   their distribution, factors which are themselves heavily dependent on
   meteorological variations. Analysis of traffic generated pollutants has
   been performed at one busy street in Newcastle upon Syne, Northeast
   England. Data interpretation is being aided by the use of Computational
   Fluid Dynamics (CFD) Modelling for this particular scenario, where a
   time based model has been used to explore the dispersion of several key
   traffic pollutants. The modeled results have been compared to measured
   pollutant concentrations.},
