   Urban Hort; Univ Catania, Dept Agr, Food \& Environm; Natl Res Council,
   Res Inst Terrestrial Ecosystems; Int Soc Hort Sci, Working Grp Landscape
   Hort},
Abstract = {Fruits are vital components of a healthy diet and should be consumed
   daily to help prevent non-communicable lifestyle diseases. However,
   fruit consumption in the Caribbean is below the World Health
   Organization recommended minimum intake of 400 g day(-1) of fruit and
   vegetables. Home gardens may offer an opportunity for cultivating fruit
   species, which can contribute to household food and nutrition security.
   The aim of this study was to create an inventory of fruit species
   diversity, uses and home gardeners' knowledge and perception of growing
   practices in Trinidad and Tobago. Home gardeners attributed several
   benefits to the production of fruit species, with the main reason being
   a source of household food and nutrition (97.2\%). A total of 55 fruit
   species, belonging to 29 plant families were reported and diversity in
   home gardens was generally high. The mean number of fruit species per
   home garden (9.44 SD +/- 8.13) and the mean number of plants per home
   garden (21.03 SD +/- 1.65) both varied significantly among regions
   (p<0.001). The study highlights the multipurpose role of fruit species
   in home gardens. Strategies that could improve fruit species diversity
   and production including providing quality planting materials and crop
   management knowledge were also proposed.},
