Note = {6th International Conference on Applied Psychology, the Person and the
   Technologies of the 21st Century, Timisoara, ROMANIA, OCT 08-11, 2009},
Organization = {Uniunea Fundatia Augusta},
Abstract = {This work aims at establishing the importance of the personal autonomy
   within risk involving professional situations as a potential factor in
   the performance achievement at work and in the prevention of accidents.
   Three characteristics (factors) of the personal autonomy were analyzed
   both in the decisional-executive professions and in the executive ones.
   (Todea, V.G. 1994)
   This research is based on the P.A. questionnaire for the assessment of
   the personal autonomy (Albu, M 2006) by means of which we tried to
   establish if there are any possible differences in the personal autonomy
   of the decisional-executive functions (engine driver, terminal operator,
   etc.) and the mainly executive functions (railroad switchman, carriage
   examiner, etc.).
   According to the hypotheses made, we studied two samples of people - one
   with executive decisional functions - lot A (N=89, of which 79 men and
   10 women, with an age average of 41.5 years) and another one consisting
   of persons with mainly executive functions - lot B (N=81, of which 44
   men and 37 women, with an age average of 42.8 years), chosen according
   to the classification criterion of the professions performed in some
   professional environments of an increased risk (Todea, V.G. 1994)
   This led us to the submission of three hypotheses of this research. The
   samples served for the verification of the hypotheses; they made
   reference to any possible differences existing between the two lots with
   regard to the personal autonomy, emotional autonomy and cognitive
   autonomy. We tried to check out if there are any major differences
   between the two categories of people. The results of the comparative
   research led to the partial confirmation of the hypotheses except for
   that referring to the cognitive autonomy, and this explained the
   conducting of the research on a large sample of people, as well as the
   computerization of the test which depended on the technology upgrading
   of transports.},
