   Hong Kong Inst Util Specialists},
Abstract = {Compared to other aspects of environmental sanitation, such as solid
   waste and toilet wastewater, greywater traditionally receives least
   attention. Whereas, in term of quantity, 68-75 \% of human water
   consumption daily will produce greywater. In urban areas in less
   developed countries, like Indonesia, greywater most often discharged
   untreated into stormwater or sewer, if exist, from where it mainly flows
   into aquatic system. In many areas, where inappropriate sewer
   constructed, greywater stop flowing, thereby leading to negative impacts
   on public health and living condition. This paper focus on greywater
   production and reuse in Bandung City. It has population around 2,364,312
   inhabitants, making it the third most populous city in Indonesia (2007).
   Expanding city, escalating water scarcity, food insecurity, livelihood
   needs, are all driving the increasing demand for untreated and treated
   wastewater for agriculture. Nevertheless, the limited space that
   available, pathogen and chemicals content in greywater requires
   appropriate model of urban agriculture, such as vertical garden and sub
   surface irrigation. Reuse greywater for food production can provide
   benefits in terms of social, economic, environmental and water
   conservation.},
